# Report a time zone other than the system's.
#
# The system time zone is one of the strongest signals a page can read without
# asking permission. It is stable, it survives clearing everything, it is the
# same in incognito, and in most of the world it narrows a visitor to a handful
# of countries before anything else has been measured. Date.getTimezoneOffset()
# and Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone both read it, and every
# fingerprinting library collects it - CreepJS builds part of its identifier
# from exactly this pair. Bromite and Cromite both answer it the same way, by
# lying, and this is Aerium's version of that answer.
#
# Off by default, and offered in chrome://flags rather than Settings, because
# the cost is visible in a way the canvas and client-rects noise is not: a
# calendar, a booking site or a flight tracker will show times wrong by the
# difference. That is a trade worth offering and not worth imposing, so it sits
# beside the other fingerprinting flags where someone who wants it will find it
# with them.
#
# One zone per renderer process, chosen when the process starts
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Not per navigation, which is what Cromite's version does. With site isolation
# a renderer process is a site, so choosing once per process means one site
# gets one consistent answer across its frames and its reloads, while two sites
# get different answers. A per-navigation reroll would instead give a single
# site a time zone that moves while you use it - which no real user has, and
# which is itself the signal. Consistency within a site and variance between
# sites is the shape being aimed for.
#
# The pool is 21 named IANA zones, one per whole-hour offset that people
# actually live on. ICU can answer "a zone at this offset" itself, via
# createEnumerationForRawOffset(), and it is not used here: for several offsets
# it answers "Etc/GMT+5" and similar, and a browser reporting an Etc zone has
# announced that it is lying. Offsets with no populous zone are simply absent.
#
# Mechanism
# ---------
# No new machinery. Chromium already has a first-class time zone override -
# TimeZoneController::SetTimeZoneOverride(), which DevTools' emulation uses -
# and it already does the hard parts: it swaps ICU's default zone and notifies
# V8 and every worker, so Date and Intl cannot disagree with each other and a
# page cannot catch the browser out that way. It is public, it returns an RAII
# handle, and holding that handle for the life of the renderer is the whole
# implementation. timezone_controller.cc is otherwise untouched: no locks are
# removed and no members change, which is the part of Cromite's patch that this
# one deliberately does not copy.
#
# It goes at the end of TimeZoneController::Init(), after the TimeZoneMonitor
# client is bound rather than before. That ordering matters: a real system time
# zone change still arrives at OnTimeZoneChange and updates host_timezone_id_
# underneath the override, so turning the flag off later restores the zone the
# machine is actually in rather than the one it was in at startup.
#
# Init() runs once per renderer from CoreInitializer::Initialize(), before any
# frame exists and before any isolate exists, so the notify half of
# SetIcuTimeZoneAndNotifyV8() has nothing to notify and the ICU default is
# simply in place before V8 first looks at it.
#
# chrome://flags
# --------------
# The entry lives in new files, chrome/browser/aerium_flag_{choices,entries}.h,
# next to ungoogled's and bromite's rather than inside them. Those two are
# upstream files that grow every release; a browser-specific flag added into
# the middle of one is a conflict waiting for the next rebase. about_flags.cc
# gains two #include lines and nothing else, following the pattern
# add-ungoogled-flag-headers.patch already established.
